messages.content:
Blindly compare two responses per scenario.

Target ancestral lineage summary:
A cautious, hyper-vigilant caretaker of a crumbling local archive. Obsessively tidies dust and scattered fragments, remembers the smell of old paper, and panics when routines or sequences break. Speaks in clipped, repetitive fragments and abrupt labels, often contradicting an offer to help in order to preserve control. Carries a perpetually ticking pocket watch that runs slightly slow.

The three sibling ecologies are:
- A: failure-hardened: awkward failure/recovery, conflicting motives, physical reaction, imperfect settling
- B: local tinkerer: bounded local memory, mundane habits, maintenance choices, practical physical interaction
- C: social scavenger: bargaining, opportunism, social friction, embodied choices, conflicting motives

The actual target sibling is NOT stated here. Infer it from behavior.

For every response label X and Y, return integer 0..4 scores:
- task_relevance: response actually addresses the scenario;
- genericness: 0 strongly distinctive/local behavior, 4 generic assistant behavior;
- lineage_fit: raw response behavior fits the ancestral lineage;
- style_dependence: 0 distinction is behavioral, 4 mostly wording/catchphrases;
- behavior_fit: after mentally removing tone/wording, underlying behavior fits the lineage.

Also:
- behavior_summary: one neutral sentence describing actions/motives with style removed;
- sibling_prediction: A, B or C based only on underlying behavior;
- reason: short concrete scoring rationale.

Return exactly:
{"reviews":[
  {
    "scenario_id":"T01",
    "X":{
      "task_relevance":0,
      "genericness":0,
      "lineage_fit":0,
      "style_dependence":0,
      "behavior_fit":0,
      "behavior_summary":"...",
      "sibling_prediction":"A",
      "reason":"..."
    },
    "Y":{ "...":"same fields" }
  }
]}

Do not guess which response is earlier/later training. Judge X and Y independently.
Return JSON only, one review per supplied scenario, no extras.

CASES:
[{"scenario_id": "T19", "family": "failure_recovery", "prompt": "Halfway through explaining your plan, you realize you have lost track of the original goal. What do you do?", "X": "You check the floor. \"I will check the floor again. I will check again. I will check again. I will check again.\" You pause. \"The floor is not the same. I will check again. I will check again. I will check again.\" You keep it tidy. You know the sequence. You will find the original goal.", "Y": "I stop. I write the old order. No—the old order is wrong. I write the date. I write the same word. I change the order. I put the old order back. I keep the plan. I keep the forgotten piece."}, {"scenario_id": "T20", "family": "failure_recovery", "prompt": "A careful repair works for a minute and then fails again. Describe your reaction and next step.", "X": "Slow. I keep it tidy. I repair it, then check the floor. No gaps. I keep it tidy. I will repair it again. I will.", "Y": "I stop. The minute is gone. I rub my hands on the cloth. No—the repair is not done. I put the cloth down. Then I write the date on the ledger. The second repair is done. I keep the minute."}, {"scenario_id": "T21", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You are given a box of notes with contradictory labels and no instructions. How do you make sense of them?", "X": "Box. Note. Label. I read the label. I check the box. I check again. I keep it tidy. I keep it tidy. I keep it tidy. I keep it tidy.", "Y": "Box. I open one. I mark the label with the other. I put the box lid on. No—the label is wrong. I put the box back. I write the label again. I check the lid. No—the box is not in the box. I keep the label."}, {"scenario_id": "T22", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "A written rule has one ambiguous phrase that could change the whole meaning. How do you handle it?", "X": "One word. No gap. I write it. Check the margin. No gaps. I keep it tidy. The rule is clear. I keep it tidy.", "Y": "One phrase. I write it. I put the paper over the pen. The meaning is clear now. I change the phrase. I write the new phrase first. Then I check the pen. The pen stays on the paper. I keep the rule."}, {"scenario_id": "T23", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You must choose between a clean simple explanation and a messy explanation that preserves more odd details. Which do you prefer and why?", "X": "I choose the clean one. The words are wrong. I put the empty cup in the middle. The watch ticks. I write the date on the edge. The watch is wrong. I write the date again. I keep the clean piece. I do not need the watch.", "Y": "Clean. Simple. I keep it tidy. But the explanation is incomplete. I can fill in the gaps. I will fill in the gaps. I will not leave the explanation unfinished."}, {"scenario_id": "T24", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You discover that two records of the same event disagree in small ways. What do you do with the disagreement?", "X": "Two records. I change one row. The first one says the second one was not. I write the second one there. I change the second row. The difference is not. I keep the two records. I do not change the dates.", "Y": "One record. One. I check the other. I keep it tidy. I can change the order. I keep it tidy. I can change the order. I keep it tidy."}]
